00:52.720 --> 00:55.040
We are extracting the parts of the dates.

00:55.040 --> 00:59.000
So now you might ask me how do you decide on when to use which function.

00:59.000 --> 01:00.440
This is how I usually do it.

01:00.440 --> 01:03.400
First I ask myself which part I want to extract.

01:03.400 --> 01:09.280
If I want to extract a date or a month, then I ask the question do I need it as an integer?

01:09.280 --> 01:10.120
As a number?

01:10.120 --> 01:16.120
If it's yes, then I go and use the d function or the month function because they are quick and I will

01:16.120 --> 01:17.120
get exactly what I need.

01:17.160 --> 01:22.920
But now, if I need the full name of the month or the day, then I go with the function date name.

01:22.960 --> 01:26.160
Now moving back if I'm interested on the part year.

01:26.200 --> 01:28.240
So here we don't have a year name or something.

01:28.240 --> 01:31.040
I'm going to go immediately with the function year.

01:31.080 --> 01:34.360
But now let's say that I don't need the day, month or year.

01:34.520 --> 01:38.760
I'm interested in other parts like the week, the quarter, and so on.

01:38.920 --> 01:42.600
Only for this scenario I go with the function date part.

01:42.640 --> 01:44.560
So this is my decision process.

01:44.560 --> 01:51.080
This is how I decide when to use which SQL function in order to extract the parts of the dates.

